seesaw-mechanism-mass-relation-ident-3ff.interpretation:
The seesaw mechanism provides a compelling explanation for the tiny masses of neutrinos compared to other fundamental fermions. In the Type I seesaw model, the standard particle content is extended to include right-handed neutrinos, $
u_R$, which are singlets under the Standard Model gauge group. This allows for two types of mass terms in the Lagrangian: a Dirac mass term connecting left- and right-handed neutrinos with mass $M_D$, generated by the Higgs mechanism, and a Majorana mass term for the right-handed neutrinos with a very large mass $M_R$. The full mass matrix in the basis $(
u_L, 
u_R^c)$ is given by $M =  egin{pmatrix} 0 & M_D \ M_D^T & M_R \end{pmatrix}$. Assuming the Majorana mass is much larger than the Dirac mass, $M_R \gg M_D$, this matrix can be block-diagonalized. The diagonalization yields two mass eigenvalues: one very heavy state with mass $\approx M_R$ (mostly the right-handed neutrino) and one very light state with an effective mass matrix $m_{
u} \approx -M_D M_R^{-1} M_D^T$. The inverse dependence on the large mass $M_R$ naturally suppresses the light neutrino mass $m_{
u}$, thus 'seesawing' it to a very small value.
